bf16 activation kernels (ReLU / SiLU / GELU) on the NPU against Zen 4 torch -- the timing
half of a result this repo had only ever half-taken. `mlir_aie_ml_examples_npu.log`
recorded all three as `PASS!` in 2026-09-07, i.e. they compile and they are correct, but
nothing was ever timed, so there was no verdict. There is one now, and it is negative:
**the NPU loses to the CPU at every op and every size measured, end to end.** The single
crossing anywhere in the grid is ReLU at 16.7M elements on NPU-time-only (1.09x), which
still loses at 0.77x once the dispatch is paid.
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), DESKTOP-CBL5NUA, 2026-09-09, mlir-aie v1.4.2,
ironenv (torch 2.14.0+cpu, 8 threads). 15 rows, each in its own process, 21:18-21:32.
`xrt-smi examine -r aie-partitions` read "No hardware contexts running on device" before
and after. The host was quiet: the peer process that had been busy earlier in the evening
(PID 22396) accumulated **33 CPU-seconds across the whole 13-minute sweep**, i.e. ~0.04
cores, and both process snapshots are in the raw output below.

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============
The backlog item is "BF16 Continuous Activation Engine: implement vector BF16
polynomial/lookup activation kernels (GELU, SiLU, Sigmoid) on AIE vector units,
eliminating Quark's HardSwish/HardSigmoid structural modifications." mlir-aie already
ships those kernels (`aie.iron.kernels.{relu,silu,gelu}`, driven by `ml/eltwise_unary`),
and this machine had already run them clean. So the open question was never "can it be
built" -- it was "is it worth dispatching", which is a timing question nobody had asked.

The prior is bad, and it is worth stating up front rather than discovering it in the
table. An activation is pure bandwidth: two bytes in, two bytes out, one cheap op per
element, zero reuse. There is no arithmetic intensity for the array to exploit, which is
the same structural reason `groupnorm_bf16` ultimately failed. And this repo has already
measured the host-set dispatch floor -- **617 us one-shot through `@iron.jit`, ~531 us
batched IRON, 36.7 us batched C++** (`dispatch_floor_npu.log`) -- so an activation only
wins if the CPU needs longer than that for the same tensor. The sweep exists to find that
crossover or to show there isn't one.

WHAT WAS RUN
============
`kernels/bf16_activation_sweep/npu_activation_sweep.py` (new here), driving the upstream
`ml/eltwise_unary` design (v1.4.2, UNMODIFIED -- no patch this time) through its own
`@iron.jit` entry point, timed with mlir-aie's own `aie.utils.benchmark.run_iters`,
20 timed iterations after 5 warmup, `num_channels=2` (both shim DMA channels per column).
The CPU leg runs in the same process, so both legs share a sitting by construction.

  python kernels/bf16_activation_sweep/npu_activation_sweep.py \
      --ops <op> --lengths <L> --iters 20 --warmup 5     # once per (op, L)

Two methodology choices, both forced by mistakes this sweep made first:

1. **More than one CPU kernel per op, verdict to the fastest.** Timing only torch's bf16
   path would have inverted the SiLU verdict completely -- see METHODOLOGY below. Each op
   is timed against 3-4 CPU implementations (bf16 and fp32 torch, plus a hand-composed
   variant) and the FASTEST is the verdict line, which is the rule
   `int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log` already set for int8 GEMM.
2. **One (op, length) per FRESH process.** Running all variants in one process let earlier
   ones warm the allocator for later ones -- also below.

Accuracy is checked against `aie.iron.kernels.{relu,silu,gelu}_ref` at the design's OWN
tolerances (relu exact; silu rtol=0.128; gelu rtol=0.128 + atol=0.05).

RESULT
======
CPU/NPU > 1 means the NPU is faster. `NPU us` is the bracket around `kernel.wait()`;
`e2e us` is the bracket around the Python call and therefore pays the dispatch -- e2e is
the honest column for "should this op run on the NPU at all".

  op     L          NPU us    e2e us    CPU us   NPU GB/s  CPU GB/s  CPU/NPU  CPU/e2e  CPU kernel
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  relu      65536    185.0     711.0       4.2       1.42     62.42     0.02     0.01  torch fp32
  relu     262144    196.6     727.9       7.2       5.33    145.64     0.04     0.01  torch fp32
  relu    1048576    239.1     724.6      22.7      17.54    184.77     0.09     0.03  torch fp32
  relu    4194304    466.9    1044.8     136.4      35.93    123.00     0.29     0.13  torch bf16
  relu   16777216   1280.0    1808.4    1390.6      52.43     48.26     1.09     0.77  clamp bf16
  silu      65536    198.4     741.2      21.4       1.32     12.22     0.11     0.03  torch fp32
  silu     262144    267.9     788.9      27.1       3.91     38.69     0.10     0.03  torch fp32
  silu    1048576    542.0    1157.2     104.7       7.74     40.08     0.19     0.09  torch fp32
  silu    4194304   1648.2    2388.3     543.0      10.18     30.90     0.33     0.23  torch fp32
  silu   16777216   5994.0    6773.9    3044.6      11.20     22.04     0.51     0.45  torch fp32
  gelu      65536    204.2     725.5      17.3       1.28     15.15     0.08     0.02  torch fp32
  gelu     262144    301.7     840.6      20.9       3.48     50.05     0.07     0.02  torch bf16
  gelu    1048576    682.3    1206.0      98.2       6.15     42.73     0.14     0.08  torch bf16
  gelu    4194304   2235.0    2998.5     360.2       7.51     46.58     0.16     0.12  torch bf16
  gelu   16777216   8354.5    9218.6    1714.6       8.03     39.14     0.21     0.19  torch bf16

**The verdict is a clean loss.** Best case end-to-end is 0.77x (ReLU, 16.7M elements =
64 MB in, 64 MB out). SiLU tops out at 0.45x, GELU at 0.19x.

Three things the table shows about WHY, in decreasing order of confidence.

1. **The NPU's ceiling is set by the op, not by the transfer, for two of the three.**
   ReLU reaches **52.43 GB/s** and is still climbing at the largest size; SiLU flattens at
   **11.20 GB/s** and GELU at **8.03 GB/s**, and both are flat from 4M to 16.7M while ReLU
   is not. All three move exactly the same bytes. So the LUT-based ops are limited on the
   core, not on the DMA -- ReLU is the only one of the three that is actually a bandwidth
   test, and it is the only one that ever crosses.

2. **The dispatch floor is the whole story at small sizes and never stops mattering.**
   e2e minus NPU time is 500-560 us at every ReLU row, which is the 531-617 us IRON floor
   this repo already measured, arriving again from an unrelated design. At L=65536 the CPU
   finishes in 4.2 us -- 170x inside the floor. Nothing that touches this dispatch path can
   win at that size regardless of how good the kernel is.

3. **The CPU is fastest exactly where the NPU is weakest.** CPU bandwidth peaks at
   184.77 GB/s at 1M elements (2 MB in, 2 MB out -- comfortably cache-resident) and falls
   to 22-48 GB/s at 16.7M when it has to go to DRAM. The NPU's only crossing happens at
   the one size where the CPU has lost its cache advantage.

ACCURACY -- the one clearly positive result here
================================================
All 15 rows PASS at the design's own tolerances, and by a wide margin:

  relu   max abs err 0.00000   exact
  silu   max abs err 0.03125   max rel err 5.48%  (tolerance allows 12.8%)
  gelu   max abs err 0.01562   max rel err 3.98%  (tolerance allows 12.8% + 0.05 abs)

Both max-abs figures are exact bf16 quantization steps (1/32 and 1/64 at these
magnitudes), which suggests the observed error is dominated by bf16 rounding rather than
by LUT approximation. So the premise behind the backlog item -- that a real bf16
SiLU/GELU on the array would be more faithful than Quark's HardSwish/HardSigmoid
substitution -- **is supported on accuracy**. It just cannot be delivered profitably
through this dispatch path.

Note on the relative-error metric: a naive max-relative-error reads exactly 1.0000 for
GELU, which is an artefact, not a finding. GELU's reference is a small negative number in
the tail (gelu(-3) ~ -0.0036), so a kernel returning 0 there scores 100% relative error
while being off by 0.0036 absolute. That is precisely why upstream pairs rtol with atol
for GELU. The metric here restricts the ratio to entries where |ref| >= 0.1 and reports
max-absolute alongside; an earlier pass of this sweep published the misleading 1.0000 and
it was corrected before this log was written.

METHODOLOGY -- two ways this sweep was wrong before it was right
===============================================================
Both are recorded because each would have produced a confident, publishable, WRONG number.

**1. CPU kernel choice swings the SiLU verdict by 4x.** At L=16.7M, isolated:

  torch fp32 silu      3044.6 us   <- verdict
  x*sigmoid bf16       4285.2 us
  x*sigmoid fp32       7455.0 us
  torch bf16 silu     11820.4 us   <- 3.9x slower than torch's own fp32 silu

Timing only `F.silu` on a bf16 tensor -- the obvious choice, matching the NPU's dtype --
would have given **CPU/NPU 1.97x and a headline "SiLU is an NPU win"**. Against the
fastest available CPU kernel it is **0.51x, a 2x loss**. torch's bf16 SiLU is an
unoptimized path on this CPU, and matching the NPU's dtype is not the same as giving the
CPU its best shot. This is the THIRD time this repo has hit this exact trap
(`conv2x_int8_cpu_baseline.log`, the MobileViT splice, now here), and the first time the
rule caught it prospectively rather than in review.

**2. Running variants in one process contaminated a timing by 5x.** In a combined pass
that timed all CPU variants for all ops in one interpreter, torch-bf16 SiLU at L=16.7M
read **2606.2 us** and was selected as the fastest CPU kernel. Two independent isolated
runs of that same call read **11820.4 us** and **12904.4 us**. The 2.6 ms figure is the
outlier and is not reproducible; the most likely cause is allocator/page reuse warmed by
the variants timed before it, though that is a hypothesis and was not isolated further.
Every row above therefore comes from its own fresh process. **UNEXPLAINED, kept as such**
-- what matters operationally is that same-process variant sweeps are not safe for
absolute CPU timings here.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
============================
- **Not a statement about the kernels' quality.** These are mlir-aie's own activation
  kernels used as shipped. Whether a hand-written bf16 activation could beat 11 GB/s is
  untested; what the table shows is that even the ReLU path, which reaches 52 GB/s and is
  presumably near the DMA limit, still loses end-to-end.
- **Not a fused result, and that is the live question.** Every row here is a STANDALONE
  dispatch, which is the worst possible framing for an op this cheap. The item's real
  value was always as an epilogue fused into a larger kernel -- exactly what
  `docs/SILICON.md` K4 says about tiny ops ("only worth building inside a larger fused
  graph, not as a standalone kernel that lands under the dispatch floor"). Nothing here
  measures a fused activation, and nothing here argues against one; `ml/
  mm_activation_epilogue` is the shape that would answer it and is Strix-only in this tree.
- **Not Sigmoid.** The item names GELU, SiLU and Sigmoid; `ml/eltwise_unary` offers
  relu/silu/gelu only, so Sigmoid was not measured.
- **No iGPU leg.** DirectML was not run; the comparison is NPU vs CPU only.

